WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.520
Film Courage: Did you see the Jim Carrey Documentary
[referring to Netflix’s Jim &amp; Andy: The

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:03.520
Great Beyond]?

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:04.520
Adam William Ward, Actor/Producer: Which one?

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.920
I saw one where he did the Man on the Moon
and he talked about…that one?

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.690
Film Courage: Yes, (I’m pretty sure) he
talks about his Dad?

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:11.820
His father was an accountant?

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:16.650
Adam: He did talk about his Dad…so his Dad
gave up his dreams to be a comedian in the

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.849
Jim Carrey documentary and he did the 9-to-5.

00:00:19.849 --> 00:00:23.050
The 9-to-5 which was a steady job laid him
off.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:28.660
So he realized at that point that there was
no safe bet and that is true man.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:29.949
It’s completely true.

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:34.190
I see that all the time with people where
they really hold onto their 9-to-5 job but

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.330
nothing’s guaranteed, nothing’s guaranteed.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:39.190
So it’s like you’ve got to take chances.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:40.190
You’ve got to.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:41.980
But that’s life.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.790
You’ve got to make mistakes too and that’s
why a lot of people…I know so many filmmakers

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:51.020
that aren’t willing to do their first feature
because they are too scared to make a mistake.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.400
It’s like No, you’ve got to make it and
if you fail you fail or if you make a movie

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.489
that’s not the best it can do, great learn
from it and make another film.

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:04.600
But I know more than one director that is
phenomenal that has never done a feature film

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:05.860
and I’ve known them for 10 years.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.840
Film Courage: Do you think it’s the money
aspect?

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.140
I can see why that would be scary.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:11.579
What do you think?

00:01:11.579 --> 00:01:14.469
Adam: I think people have all kinds of excuses.

00:01:14.469 --> 00:01:18.539
I think money is kind of the number one excuse
is I don’t have enough money or I can’t

00:01:18.539 --> 00:01:21.740
get enough money to make the feature but I
mean what are you doing to try to get the

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:22.740
money?

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:23.810
There is that aspect.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:25.899
A lot of them go “I don’t have the right
script.”

00:01:25.899 --> 00:01:29.709
And I’m not a script writer, a lot of that
kind of stuff.

00:01:29.709 --> 00:01:35.299
And that’s one of the reasons that WALLY
GOT WASTED…it was like Seth my writing partner

00:01:35.299 --> 00:01:41.329
Seth Hymes basically he wrote a script and
came to me and I didn’t really particularly

00:01:41.329 --> 00:01:42.329
like it.

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:45.909
I liked the first 20 pages and I was like
the first 20 pages was slightly similar to

00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:47.829
WALLY GOT WASTED.

00:01:47.829 --> 00:01:51.960
And I was like “I thought the movie was
going in this direction.”

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:55.939
Because in the first three pages it’s about
three who accidentally hurt somebody and then

00:01:55.939 --> 00:01:59.479
the movie went in a totally different direction
and I was like I really thought you were going

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:05.319
to reamp WEEKEND AT BERNIES and put the HANGOVER
together and put this new kind of comedy movie

00:02:05.319 --> 00:02:09.040
because that’s what we ended up doing and
he was like “No.”

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.920
And after he was heartbroken for awhile because
I told him my honest opinion and he came back

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:17.710
and was like “Well let’s write the idea
that you have.”

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:24.700
And so that was like 2014 and so we were living
together at the time (we were roommates) and

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:32.680
I laid out the whole movie in one night with
him and I came up with the structure of it

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:33.690
and we didn’t end up writing it.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:37.710
He ended up moving out later and then 6 months
later he came back and he wrote a first draft

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:41.670
in a day or two because he’s a very fast
writer.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:46.260
There was one scene in the first draft which
was the drive-through scene in the movie which

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:52.570
was phenomenal which was great because he
did my outline so it had the correct outline,

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:55.540
it just wasn’t as good as it needed to be
yet.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.370
But it was nice because I always try to stick
on the positive because any artist is very

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:03.140
sensitive usually and so “I was like listen
this is phenomenal.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.480
But the driving through scene is amazing.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.540
And the whole movie needs to be as good as
the drive through scene.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:09.730
So we need to do rewrites.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:18.430
So then we ended up doing rewrites for eight
or nine months to a year on the script and

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:20.890
getting it to where we thought it was strong.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:26.220
And then I had enough connections in Hollywood
that I was sending it to anyone who would

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:31.110
help me make it at the time which is where
most every filmmaker ends up dying because

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:35.880
they are trying to hand their project to someone
else to help them make it and that’s usually

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.690
falling on silent ears.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:42.770
So we did that and there was one studio that
was thinking about making it and it was just

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:47.960
talk and I was like “We can’t keep doing
this.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:52.280
Let’s just do it ourselves.”

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:57.750
So we ended up raising the money ourselves
selling units to the movie for a certain dollar

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:02.730
amount you owned a percentage of the movie
and I was going to sell units to anyone I

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:03.740
could sell units to.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:08.330
I sold them to doctors, I sold them to dentists,
I sold them to somebody in France, I sold

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:10.319
them to somebody in Mississippi.

00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:13.950
I sold them anywhere I could sell them and
talked to people.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:17.329
And talking to people and convincing them
to hand you a check is never easy.

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:21.670
I alway tell people I’m one of the luckiest
people you’ll ever meet and people seem

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:24.960
to like me and trust me so I’m lucky in
that aspect.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:25.960
But I deliver!

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:29.280
I delivered the movie like I promised I would.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.150
We raised the money and it took like a year
or a year and a half to raise some of the

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:37.560
money (not all of it).

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.030
Film Courage: $70,000?

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:43.560
Adam: Well…$70,000 yes but Seth [Hymes]
my partner was actually coming up with $40,000

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:48.139
of that actually but we only raised like $30,000
of it.

00:04:48.139 --> 00:04:50.600
And unfortunately Seth was in a bad business
deal.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:55.200
And I actually did not know that so I was
like “We have the $70,000, we can start.”

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.460
I did all of the casting of the movie already.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:02.130
I found 38 locations for free in Los Angeles.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.520
That’s like unheard of right?

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.730
Film Courage: How did you do that?

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:09.390
Adam: Meeting…well we have drive-throughs,
we have grocery stores, we have fast food

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:11.780
restaurants, we have night clubs.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:16.320
It took time and I didn’t want to put dates
down for the film until I found all my locations

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:17.470
and found all of my actors.

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:21.130
I did not want to have a date because I was
working every day anyway.

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:25.460
I didn’t need a time to push me.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:37.400
So every location that I needed and I would
be like “Hey can we use this space to shoot?

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.340
Can I speak to the manager and talk to them?”

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:44.190
Chains would all turn me down “It’s a
corporate decision.”

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:49.730
So I learned very quickly that I needed to
find Mom-and-Pop places, Mom-and-Pop grocery

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:52.390
stores, Mom-and-Pop fast food places.

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:55.210
Basically I just needed to be able talk to
the person who was making the decision and

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:59.910
not have a million lines to get to who I need
to talk.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:03.000
And it was just convincing people with passion
really.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.500
It was my first feature.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.750
My butt was on the line.

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:15.220
I think they saw how much excitement I had
and a lot of people just agreed to help me.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:20.050
And then sometimes it was a connection through
somebody else, like at the grocery store,

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:24.870
it was a connection through Seth, it was a
friend of his from college who owned a grocery

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:27.710
story and they gave us permission to shoot
there.

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:30.480
And they we went there on the day and they
didn’t know who we were.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:34.810
Somebody didn’t even tell them but they
saw the camera and the crew and they were

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:36.920
excited and they ended up letting us shoot
there.

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.431
I don’t even know if they even needed permission
to shoot there because they didn’t know

00:06:39.431 --> 00:06:41.080
who we were.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:44.669
And we shot at the grocery store when it was
open.

00:06:44.669 --> 00:06:50.500
Some of my actors were joking and we have
a 35-minute documentary on YouTube (actually

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:54.280
people can type in WALLY GOT WASTED behind-the-scenes
and it will pop right up).

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:57.810
It’s a little 35-minute documentary on how
we made the film.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:02.500
But one of the actors in the documentary is
like “Live theater” because there are

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:06.450
homeless people walking by, there are regular
people walking by in the middle of the shot

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:11.620
when we were shooting but we made it work.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:17.650
So yes just convincing every location to just
let us shoot there.

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:23.050
And some of the locations would back out the
day before because the fast food restaurant

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:28.300
was like “I need to come there tomorrow
night.”

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:30.820
And he was like “You can come after we close.”

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:31.820
And it closes at 10:00.

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:34.300
And he says “How long does you need to be
here?”

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:36.710
He’s thinking I need to be like an hour.

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:39.419
No we need to shoot until the sun comes up.

00:07:39.419 --> 00:07:44.290
I need as much time as possible so normally
we’ll try to shoot 12 hours but I have the

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:45.290
sun.

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:48.080
So we’ll shoot from 10:00 p.m. until 5:00
a.m.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:51.840
And he’s like “Oh…I have to stay here
until 5:00 a.m.?”

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.180
He was immediately like I don’t want to
do this anymore and I was like “Listen you

00:07:56.180 --> 00:08:01.040
can’t bail on me I have nothing, I’m completely
screwed.”

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:07.700
So I would convince him to stay basically
and that would happen a lot.

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:14.229
Rewind all the way back to Seth getting that
money stolen from him, so I thought we were

00:08:14.229 --> 00:08:22.360
shooting the movie for $70,000 and rewind…Seth
was going to put in $40,000…the $40,000

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:27.110
was stolen so he didn’t know until the first
day of shooting.

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:31.510
So basically the first day of shooting and
you see photos and videos of him and you can

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:36.789
see he’s having a nervous breakdown, his
eyes are like half open because he still has

00:08:36.789 --> 00:08:39.630
to tell me that he’s lost all of that money.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:44.010
We have our first day of shooting and we finally
get back (because he moved back in my apartment

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:45.110
to shoot the movie.

00:08:45.110 --> 00:08:53.260
And he comes into my room after like 14 hours
and he was like completely destroyed and he’s

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.310
like “I lost all of the money, we have to
shut the movie down.”

00:08:55.310 --> 00:09:00.070
And I said “Seth I’ve got to tell you
that there is nothing in the world that could

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:01.960
stop me from shooting this movie at this point.

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:03.930
No, we’re not going to stop.

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.640
We’ll use the $30,000 and we’ll put the
rest on credit cards.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:09.050
Me and you will open credit cards and we’re
going to finish the movie.

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:13.390
We will put everything we possibly can on
credit cards but we will not stop the movie.”

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:16.240
And when he came into my room he was like
“We’re going to shut down the movie and

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:19.530
I’m going to move away from Los Angeles
and I’m done with this dream.

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:20.530
I’m never going to do it again.”

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:25.810
And I was like “No, you’re going to finish
your dream and we’re going to drive forward.”

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:26.910
I wouldn’t let him quit.

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:28.880
I’m like “We have to do this.”

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:32.980
And so that’s what we did, we put the rest
of the movie, $40,000 on credit cards and

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:39.690
$30,000 we used to pay the crew and people
we needed to pay and we finished it.

